<h1 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h1>
<p>Use this page when Jundot cannot create a project, the AI cannot continue, the editor reports an error, or performance drops.</p>

<h2 id="ai-cannot-create-project">AI cannot create a project</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Check that the AI backend is configured and reachable.</li>
    <li>Describe the desired game type, theme, and minimum playable loop.</li>
    <li>Use a stable local project path.</li>
    <li>Ask the AI to summarize the plan before it writes files.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="script-errors">Script errors</h2>
<p>Copy the exact error, scene name, and reproduction step into the AI Assistant. The AI should inspect the relevant files, apply a focused fix, and ask you to run the project again.</p>

<h2 id="performance">Performance problems</h2>
<p>Share when the slowdown appears, what scene is open, and what action triggers it. The AI can inspect scripts, scene complexity, and logs before proposing a fix.</p>

<h2 id="engine-limitation">Engine limitation</h2>
<p>If the AI says the current engine cannot complete the project request, ask it to explain the boundary. A good answer states what project code cannot do, what engine change is needed, and how the user will verify it.</p>

<h2 id="github-upload-fails">GitHub upload fails</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Check that Git is installed and available.</li>
    <li>Confirm that the project has a remote repository.</li>
    <li>Verify that no secrets or generated temporary files are staged.</li>
    <li>Ask the AI to summarize the current diff before upload.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="still-blocked">Still blocked</h2>
<p>Provide the exact error text, screenshots when useful, and the last action that worked. The AI should use that evidence instead of guessing.</p>
